---
import {descriptions} from '../scripts/origins-study/descriptions.js';
import {modalReference} from '../scripts/origins-study/mineral-map.js';
import LegacyShell from '../components/LegacyShell.astro';
import {getPageScripts} from '../data/site';
---
<LegacyShell title="Origins | Mads LIU Yong" description="An interactive four-chapter study of Solar System origins and proposed Ryugu parent-body evolution." current="origins" path="origins-study.html" scriptHtml={getPageScripts('origins')}>
<div class="origins-study">
  <section class="study-intro">
    <div><p class="eyebrow">Origins</p><h1>Formation &amp; evolution</h1><p class="lead">From a disk of dust and ice to the mineral record of a smaller world.</p><p class="study-project-context">This Ryugu-based model provides comparison context for the water–rock processes investigated in my <a href="research.html#research-focus-title">Orgueil research</a>. Orgueil’s parent body is unknown.</p></div>
    <button id="evidence" class="button secondary" type="button"><i data-lucide="book-open"></i><span>Scientific context</span></button>
  </section>
  <div class="study-panel">
  <nav class="chapters" aria-label="Evolution chapters"><button data-stage="0" aria-current="step"><span>01</span><strong>Dust & ice</strong><small>A cold beginning</small></button><button data-stage="1"><span>02</span><strong>Collision & growth</strong><small>A parent takes shape</small></button><button data-stage="2"><span>03</span><strong>Water & rock</strong><small>An interior transformed</small></button><button data-stage="3"><span>04</span><strong>Breakup & inheritance</strong><small>A smaller world</small></button></nav>
  <section class="observatory" aria-label="Interactive early Solar System evolution">

    <div class="scene-frame">
      <div class="scene-frame-bar"><span><i aria-hidden="true"></i>Interactive scene</span><span id="scene-counter">01 / 04</span></div>
      <div id="viewport"><div id="fragment-guide" class="fragment-guide" hidden><span>↶ Slower debris returns</span><span>Fast ejecta escape →</span></div><canvas tabindex="0" id="scene" aria-label="Three-dimensional scientific illustration" aria-describedby="scene-hint"></canvas><div class="scene-load-state"><p id="loading" role="status">Preparing the material study...</p><button id="retry-scene" type="button" hidden>Retry scene</button></div><button id="explore" type="button" aria-pressed="false">Explore</button><span class="scene-hint" id="scene-hint">Drag or Arrow keys to orbit · Space to play/pause · Zoom with the + / − buttons</span></div>
    </div>
    <aside class="chapter-copy" aria-labelledby="title">
      <div class="chapter-heading"><span class="eyebrow" id="eyebrow">01 / THE SOLAR NEBULA</span><h2 id="title">Before the planets</h2></div>
      <div class="chapter-description"><p id="description">{descriptions[0].description}</p></div>
      <ol id="reaction-sequence" class="reaction-sequence" aria-label="Water–rock process" hidden><li><button type="button" data-process-progress="0.08" aria-label="Show Dispersed ice"><span>01</span><div><strong>Dispersed ice</strong><small>Fine grains within the matrix</small></div></button></li><li><button type="button" data-process-progress="0.27" aria-label="Show Local melting"><span>02</span><div><strong>Local melting</strong><small>Liquid wets nearby surfaces</small></div></button></li><li><button type="button" data-process-progress="0.5" aria-label="Show Water migration"><span>03</span><div><strong>Water migration</strong><small>Connected pores carry the reaction</small></div></button></li><li><button type="button" data-process-progress="0.9" aria-label="Show Matrix alteration"><span>04</span><div><strong>Matrix alteration</strong><small>Phyllosilicates dominate; carbonates are sparse</small></div></button></li></ol>
      <ol id="growth-sequence" class="reaction-sequence" aria-label="Collective growth" hidden><li><button type="button" data-process-progress="0.16" aria-label="Show Fine grains → aggregates"><span>01</span><div><strong>Fine grains → aggregates</strong><small>Many regions grow simultaneously</small></div></button></li><li><button type="button" data-process-progress="0.51" aria-label="Show Concentrate the particles"><span>02</span><div><strong>Concentrate the particles</strong><small>Collective gathering, with some losses</small></div></button></li><li><button type="button" data-process-progress="0.92" aria-label="Show Assemble a porous body"><span>03</span><div><strong>Assemble a porous body</strong><small>Fine material connects the aggregate</small></div></button></li></ol>
      <ol id="inheritance-sequence" class="reaction-sequence" aria-label="Fragment fate" hidden><li><button type="button" data-process-progress="0.12" aria-label="Show Disrupt the parent"><span>01</span><div><strong>Disrupt the parent</strong><small>A later, energetic impact</small></div></button></li><li><button type="button" data-process-progress="0.38" aria-label="Show Two fragment populations"><span>02</span><div><strong>Two fragment populations</strong><small>Fast ejecta escape; slower material expands</small></div></button></li><li><button type="button" data-process-progress="0.9" aria-label="Show Return and reaccumulate"><span>03</span><div><strong>Return and reaccumulate</strong><small>Coarse fragments and fine debris pack together</small></div></button></li></ol>
      <div class="scene-note"><span class="region-label">Reading the scene</span><strong id="scale">{descriptions[0].scale}</strong><p id="environment">{descriptions[0].environment}</p></div>
    </aside>
    <div class="scene-controls" role="group" aria-label="Scene appearance and camera">
      <div class="view-tools"><span class="region-label">View</span><div class="segmented" aria-label="Color mode"><button id="material-mode" aria-pressed="true">Material</button><button id="phase-mode" aria-pressed="false">Process</button></div><div class="camera-tools"><button class="icon" id="zoom-in" aria-label="Zoom in" title="Zoom in"><i data-lucide="zoom-in"></i></button><button class="icon" id="zoom-out" aria-label="Zoom out" title="Zoom out"><i data-lucide="zoom-out"></i></button><button class="icon" id="reset" aria-label="Reset view" title="Reset view"><i data-lucide="rotate-ccw"></i></button></div></div>
      <div class="view-key"><p id="material-note">Illustrative material appearance</p><div class="legend" id="legend" hidden></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="transport"><button id="play" class="icon" aria-label="Pause" title="Pause"><i data-lucide="pause"></i></button><div class="transport-copy"><span class="region-label">Chapter timeline</span><span id="moment">Nebular disk</span></div><input id="progress" type="range" min="0" max="1" step=".001" value=".1" aria-label="Chapter progression"><output id="progress-value">10%</output></div>

    <section id="mineral-budget" class="mineral-budget" aria-labelledby="mineral-budget-title" hidden>
      <div class="mineral-budget-heading"><div><span class="region-label">Final assemblage · sample reference</span><h3 id="mineral-budget-title">88% phyllosilicates. 1.6% carbonates.</h3></div><a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41550-022-01745-5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ryugu C0068 · Ito et al., 2022 ↗</a></div>
      <div class="mineral-budget-bar" aria-hidden="true">{modalReference.percent.map((value,i)=><span style={`flex:${value};--phase-color:${['#6c9b85','#b0915e','#6088b1','#d6ba80','#ab89b1','#273638'][i]}`}></span>)}</div>
      <dl class="mineral-budget-values">{modalReference.phases.map((label,i)=><div><dt>{label}</dt><dd>{modalReference.percent[i]}<small>%</small></dd></div>)}</dl>
      <p>Reported vol% includes 2.5% mapped voids. These values set the synthetic section’s final area fractions, not crystal counts or the initial ice content. Sulfides are mainly pyrrhotite; carbonates are mainly dolomite. No calcite/dolomite split is assigned without a measured phase split.</p>
      <details><summary>Primary minerals, alteration products &amp; the Orgueil comparison</summary><p>The dominant serpentine–saponite matrix is an aqueous alteration product. Preserved anhydrous silicates are rare: Ito et al. found about 0.5 vol% in C0009, while C0068 is reported as not detected. This scene does not multiply large primary silicate crystals throughout the altered matrix.</p><p>King et al. (2015), Table 1, reports an average of 83 vol% phyllosilicates, 5.7% sulfides and 6.7% magnetite in three Orgueil samples. Carbonates were below that study’s approximately 1 vol% detection limit; this does not mean that Orgueil contains none. Calcite and dolomite occur heterogeneously in other Orgueil material. These measurements are not substituted for the C0068 row, or claimed as measurements of my own specimen.</p><p><a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gca.2015.05.038" target="_blank" rel="noopener">King et al., 2015 · modal mineralogy</a> · <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.epsl.2009.01.038" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lee &amp; Nicholson, 2009 · Orgueil Ca-carbonate</a></p></details>
    </section>
  </section>

  </div><div class="study-caption"><p>Proposed Ryugu-parent evolution <span>/</span> Nakamura et al., 2023</p><p>Conceptual 3D illustration. Geometry, colors and timing are not a measured reconstruction.</p></div>
<dialog id="sources"><button class="icon close" aria-label="Close scientific context" title="Close"><i data-lucide="x"></i></button><span class="eyebrow">EVIDENCE & INTERPRETATION</span><h2>A model, not a recovered film.</h2><p>The sequence follows the evolution figure on the first page of Nakamura et al. The paper proposes a cold, ice-bearing parent, internal heating and aqueous alteration, followed by disruption and reaccretion.</p><dl><dt>Supported sequence</dt><dd>Accretion of rock and ice; ice melting; water-rock reactions; cooling; a later impact; preservation and reaccretion of less-heated material.</dd><dt>Artist's interpretation</dt><dd>Disk structure, collision paths, light, material colors and internal textures. Chapter 02 separates dust coagulation, particle concentration and collective assembly. These are mechanism illustrations: sticking has barriers, and rebound, erosion or fragmentation can limit growth. The gas flow, streaming instability and gravitational collapse are not numerically solved here. Nakamura et al. assume instantaneous accretion in their thermal calculation; that does not describe a sequence of seven giant mergers. Chapter 04 keeps fragment size fixed, separates escaping and returning material, and shows a new porous packing. Its precise trajectories and retained fraction are not measured or an N-body result.</dd><dt>Inside the matrix</dt><dd>Chapter 03 illustrates dispersed ice, local wetting and a predominantly phyllosilicate alteration assemblage. Its final synthetic area fractions follow the Ryugu C0068 row of Ito et al. (2022), Extended Data Table 1: 88% phyllosilicates, 2.4% sulfides, 5.3% magnetite, 1.6% carbonates, 0.2% apatite and 2.5% voids. The source reports vol% from section mapping, including voids. Angular lithic domains embedded in a finer matrix illustrate the brecciated, heterogeneous returned material described by Nakamura et al. and compared with Orgueil by Lee and Nicholson (2009, Fig. 3). This is not a claim that all brecciation preceded alteration. Domain geometry, local enrichments, pore connectivity and reaction speed are authored explanations, not a reconstruction of C0068 or a fluid-flow simulation.</dd><dt>Light & color</dt><dd>The external disk view represents scattered light. The cold midplane and interiors use illustrative lighting to make normally obscured material visible. Process colors are categorical overlays, not visible emission or a computed temperature field.</dd><dt>Scale & time</dt><dd>Chapters change scale and compress very different timescales. No measured orbital positions or snow-line radii are implied. Chapter 03 uses a sample-specific final phase budget; intermediate textures and elapsed time are not measured reaction kinetics.</dd><dt>Orgueil</dt><dd>Orgueil's parent is unknown. This Ryugu-based model is a comparison context, not an identification of Orgueil's parent body.</dd></dl><p class="source-links"><a href="https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-earth-040809-152513" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chiang &amp; Youdin, 2010 / Planetesimal formation</a><a href="https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.astro.46.060407.145152" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Blum &amp; Wurm, 2008 / Aggregate growth</a><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S001670372400156X" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tsuchiyama et al., 2024 / Three-dimensional Ryugu textures</a><a href="https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn8671" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nakamura et al. / Science</a><a href="https://global.jaxa.jp/press/2022/09/20220923-1_e.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JAXA / Stone-team results</a><a href="https://www.eso.org/public/news/eso1333/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ESO / Shielded disk midplanes</a></p></dialog>
</div>
</LegacyShell>
